{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Authorized participants (APs)** and **market makers** serve different—but often overlapping—roles:\n\n- **APs operate in the primary market.** They have agreements allowing them to create or redeem ETF shares directly with the fund, typically in large “creation units,” exchanging securities and/or cash. This expands or contracts the ETF’s share supply.\n- **Market makers operate in the secondary market.** They quote bid and ask prices on exchanges, buying and selling ETF shares to provide trading liquidity. They manage inventory and hedge exposures, seeking to earn spreads and trading profits.\n\nMarket makers can work through APs when they need shares created or redeemed. Arbitrage connecting these activities helps keep ETF prices near portfolio value, though premiums and discounts can persist.\n\n**Worth knowing:** One firm can perform both roles, but being a market maker does not automatically make it an AP."}}
